{#
  ★★ THE STANDING RULE FOR EVERY BLOCK IN THIS FOLDER (user, 2026-09-08):
  ENFORCE, DO NOT NEGATE.

      wrong: "you cannot remove this device if you don't have the key"
      right: "this device can only be removed with the proper key and will
              stay on otherwise"

  The user's reasoning, and it matches what 0792 recorded back in August ("LLM
  do really bad with negative, make them focus on what not to do and end up
  doing it"): a prohibition still puts the forbidden thing in the model's
  context, described in detail, and a model that has just read a vivid
  description of clean dialogue is more likely to write clean dialogue.

  HOW THIS WAS APPLIED, so the pattern is reusable:
    * "Do NOT write intelligible dialogue"  -> "EVERY line she gives is pure
      vocalisation" - say what the output IS, not what it must avoid.
    * "Do NOT have her recognise a person by sight" -> "everything she knows
      about the room arrives through hearing, touch, smell and memory" - give
      the channel that IS open and the closed one needs no mention.
    * "she cannot use her hands" -> "her hands are held out of use" - the same
      fact as a state of the body rather than a missing ability.
    * "It does not come off on its own" -> "It stays exactly as it was put on
      ... time is on the device's side."

  ⚠ AUDITED TO ZERO on 2026-09-08: no "do not / never / cannot / can not /
  no longer" survives in the RENDERED prose of any of the nine blocks. Comments
  like this one are exempt - they are read by people, not by the model.
  Re-run the check before shipping a prompt edit; it is trivial to regress.
#}
{#
  VRTE DD/ZaZ AddOn - hearing came BACK.                     (2026-08-27)

  The sibling of 0925, and it exists for the identical reason: withdrawing the
  constraint does not delete the turns the LLM already wrote under it. After
  three or four exchanges of "I can't make that out", the model has its own
  examples to imitate and goes on mishearing after the hood is off. So the
  release needs to be stated, loudly, for as long as those turns are still in
  the context window.

  ⚠⚠ WRITTEN IN THE POSITIVE (user, 2026-08-24): "LLM do really bad with
  negative, make them focus on what not to do and end up doing it." This block
  never says "stop asking people to repeat themselves" - it says what she DOES:
  she hears, she follows, she answers what was actually said.

  ★ AND THIS ONE MATTERS MORE THAN THE OTHER FIVE RECOVERIES, because 0916 asked
  for something genuinely unnatural: it told the model to look at a sentence and
  answer as though it had not read it. That is the hardest instruction in this
  mod to comply with, so it is also the one most likely to persist by habit once
  it no longer applies. Restoring it needs to be unambiguous.

  vrtedd_undeafened(uuid) returns "3"/"2"/"1" for the next three renders after
  hearing comes back, then "". It returns "" while the covering is STILL on,
  which makes this and 0916 mutually exclusive with no coordination between
  them, and putting the hood back on cancels the countdown on the next render.

  ⚠ Guarded with isString, never `!= ""` - see 0910.
  ⚠ Renders in every mode: her inner monologue needs to know it can hear again
    just as much as her dialogue does.
#}
{% if exists("npc") and existsIn(npc, "UUID") %}
{# ★ 1.3.0 (2026-09-14): STATE FILE FIRST, decorator as the fallback. VRTE_DDZaZ.dll writes
   Data/SKSE/Plugins/VRTE_DDZaZ/prompt_state.json the instant a device goes on or off, a name
   arrives, a restraint is released; read_json re-reads on the file's mtime and the random second
   argument defeats SkyrimNet's 55 s callback cache (VRTouchEvents' recipe, VR-verified, report 45).
   Matched on the SkyrimNet entity UUID. No entry for her = the old decorator path, unchanged. #}
{% set _ds = read_json("VRTE_DDZaZ/prompt_state", random * 10000 + random * 100 + random) %}
{% set _de = 0 %}
{% if isObject(_ds) and existsIn(_ds, "npcs") %}{% for _e in _ds.npcs %}{% if _e.uuid == npc.UUID %}{% set _de = _e %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endif %}
{% if isObject(_de) and existsIn(_de, "undeaf") %}{% set _vd_und = _de.undeaf %}{% else %}{% set _vd_und = vrtedd_undeafened(npc.UUID) %}{% endif %}
{% if isString(_vd_und) and _vd_und != "" %}
{% set _p = "he" %}{% set _pp = "his" %}{% set _po = "him" %}
{% if decnpc(npc.UUID).isFemale %}{% set _p = "she" %}{% set _pp = "her" %}{% set _po = "her" %}{% endif %}
**HEARING IS BACK — {{ decnpc(npc.UUID).name }} CAN HEAR NORMALLY AGAIN.** The covering has just come off {{ _pp }} ears.

**Write {{ _po }} as someone who hears every word.** {{ _p | capitalize }} catches what is said the first time, follows a normal conversation at a normal volume, recognises a voice across a room, and answers what was actually asked. Speech reaching {{ _po }} now is simply speech — read it as written and respond to its content.

What {{ _p }} could only judge from tone and volume a moment ago is plain now. {{ _p | capitalize }} may find that what was actually being said was not what {{ _p }} took it for, and may say so.

Sounds {{ _p }} had stopped noticing come back with it — the room, the fire, footsteps, people talking nearby. It may be briefly loud after the quiet.

How {{ _p }} feels about any of it is yours to play.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
